+// the window's title on the left, then Job H's "Cursor" toggle
+// (same objectName, wxQtIOSFloatingControlsCursorToggle, kept in
+// its bar slot relative to the buttons), then Minimize and Close
+// at the right end (Windows order, Close outermost). The strip
+// itself is the drag region -- the 0013 "Move" grip is RETIRED
+// because the whole non-button strip now arms the same
+// coordinate-based drag (0013's arming, un-maximise and
+// TouchCancel handling are unchanged). Each strip acts on its
+// OWN window, so the modal-host question of rounds 11/12 no
+// longer decides which window Close/Minimize hit. Job H's cursor
+// overlay, which rode the roaming bar's host, now rides the
+// topmost titlebar host (the modal window while one is up, else
+// the current window) -- the same window the bar would have
+// picked, picked the same way.
+//
+// The strip must not cover the host's menu bar or toolbar. wxQt
+// frames are QMainWindows (src/qt/frame.cpp): the menu bar,
+// toolbars and central widget are placed by QMainWindow's own
+// layout, and wx client children live in the central widget
+// (wxFrame::QtGetParentWidget()). For a QMainWindow host the
+// strip therefore sits in a container widget (objectName
+// wxQtIOSWindowTitleBarMenuHost) installed with
+// QMainWindow::setMenuWidget(): the container stacks the strip
+// ABOVE the window's real menu bar, QMainWindow's layout
+// reserves real space for the container, and the menu bar,
+// toolbars and client area shift down intact -- nothing is
+// overlaid. Dialogs and plain top-level widgets have no menu
+// bar and no layout slot to reserve, so their strip is a child
+// overlay at the top AND the dialog is grown by the strip
+// height with its existing children shifted down by the same
+// amount, so the strip occupies newly created space rather
+// than covering content (a later wx relayout can only undo the
+// shift, returning that dialog to a plain top overlay).
